Subject: [PATCH 07971/11362] nav-side pruning wired (default-off) and
 measured: INOPERATIVE at the eval budget, and search-WORSE where it fires

[outer-loop] HazardMovePruner is now wired into the scored E3 path behind
SUBMITTED_HAZARD_MOVE_PRUNER_ENABLED = False, added as the 8th GATED_FLAG so every arm pins it and
the drift guard covers it (verified: 8 flags, 0 arms missing one, new arm H = live config + pruner
with control G3). Orphan lint: 65 modules in the live closure.

THE DECISIVE FINDING -- the lever cannot operate in the regime that scores. The shipped agent is
capped at MAX_ACTIONS = 400 per game. Measured at budget 400, seed 20260724:
  tu93  1 death   -> NOT fitted -> UNINTERPRETABLE_NOT_FITTED
  g50t  32 deaths -> NOT fitted -> UNINTERPRETABLE_NOT_FITTED
  lp85  0 nav     -> UNINTERPRETABLE_NO_NAV (393 of 393 pointer rows correctly skipped)
It needs budget 2000 before it fits at all, where tu93 finally FIRED_AND_PRUNED (1889 nav
transitions, 13 deaths, omni rung fitted, 6 rows pruned, level preserved at 2) -- the first
demonstration this pruner fires on the SCORED path rather than the offline twin. But there it makes
search WORSE: states_expanded 136 -> 146 and wall clock 5.07s -> 137.25s (~27x, the O(n^2) refit at
refit_every=50). It RESHAPES rather than reduces search -- the same signature as the click-side
sibling (+22 states). Single-seed; do not over-read the magnitudes. The generalisable point is
structural: a lever that must LEARN from observed transitions may be inoperative at a 400-action
budget, which bears on every learn-from-experience lever this project has queued.

Legality was two-tier, not a stop: runtime is clean (rung selection is in-sample fit from the
agent own transitions, should_prune reads only the current grid, no BFS / source read /
ground-truth flag), but the omni rung PREDICATE SHAPE was outer-loop-derived -- a transfer risk,
not a hidden-game-illegal mechanism. Wired anyway, default-off, with fire-counters that make an
uninterpretable null impossible to report as a finding.

A REAL PRE-EXISTING DEFECT found while writing tests: _default_action_of_label documented that it
returns None for non-nav labels, but the shape both live consumers actually pass --
{action: 6, data: {x, y}} -- decodes to the int 6 and was buffered as a keyboard-nav transition, so
on a click-heavy game the fitter was asked to learn a displacement for a pointer action. Fixed as
OPT-IN (nav_actions_only=True) so the offline twin default stays byte-identical and the published
tu93 artifact remains reproducible; the scored-path coercion opts in. Measured: lp85 skips 393/393.

Also added a NEVER-EMPTY guard: condemning every row would empty untested -> explored_out -> the run
ends early, a mechanical null that reads as behavioural.

VERIFICATION: 133 tests pass across affected files (30 hazard-wiring + 6 harness here). Failure-SET
comparison against a worktree control with results/, ops/, openspec/, environment_files/ and models/
symlinked so only the 3 source files differed, both runs launched CONCURRENTLY so conductor churn hit
both equally: control 54 failed / 7644 passed, treatment 44 failed / 7684 passed, regressions EMPTY.
adversarial_verify on the scored-path artifact: 0 flagged.

HONEST GAP: the workflow FIX phase died on an API 529, so its two adversarial reviews reported
findings that were never applied. Treat the scored-path LLM-on numbers as PRE-REVIEW.

